{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '220,355p;500,550p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '55,225p;245,285p;380,415p'\nrg -n \\\"occurrence|lexical-context|validate-resolved|cycle|provenance|biconditional|converse|side table|side-table|supplied|synthesized|lower-stack|lower-register\\\" docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '1,280p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md | sed -n '220,355p;500,550p'\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '55,225p;245,285p;380,415p'\nrg -n \\\"occurrence|lexical-context|validate-resolved|cycle|provenance|biconditional|converse|side table|side-table|supplied|synthesized|lower-stack|lower-register\\\" docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '1,280p'\"","aggregated_output":"   220\tLowering is two stages, both pure stream-to-stream functions:\n   221\t\n   222\t    (resolve named-datoms)          -> resolved tuples + binder side table\n   223\t    (lower-stack resolved-tuples)   -> canonical stack image + pc side table\n   224\t\n   225\t`resolve` is the de Bruijn encoding of the named semantic tuples. It is the\n   226\tshared upstream of the stack projection specified here and the register\n   227\tprojection specified in `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`; neither projection\n   228\tis derived from the other, matching the parallel-projection topology in\n   229\t`docs/agents/architecture.md`. `resolve` receives complete `:yin/*` named\n   230\tdatoms and reuses only the public `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` helper.\n   231\tThat helper compares names exactly, including rightmost-wins duplicate\n   232\tparameters, and does not canonicalize values. Projection-only helpers such\n   233\tas `index-frame`, `build-node`, and `project-node` are not reused.\n   234\tUnexpanded-macro validation remains the named front end's responsibility.\n   235\tThe projection is therefore kept, not removed: if it is ever retired, this\n   236\tpublic helper must first move into the resolver's namespace.\n   237\t\n   238\t### 3.1 Resolved tuples\n   239\t\n   240\tThe named datoms are a finite graph, not a tree. `ast->datoms-with-root`\n   241\temits a node carrying a pre-assigned `:eid` exactly once and references it\n   242\tfrom every site that names it, for every node type. One source entity can\n   243\ttherefore sit under two different lexical contexts, and its resolution is\n   244\tthen different at each: the existing projection fixture\n   245\t`shared-nodes-resolve-per-their-lexical-context` places one `:variable`\n   246\tentity as the body of both `(fn [x] ...)` and `(fn [y] ...)`, bound `[0 0]`\n   247\tunder the first and free `x` under the second. Resolution is a fact about\n   248\tan occurrence, not about a source entity, so resolved tuples are keyed by\n   249\toccurrence. Writing resolution facts onto the shared source entity would\n   250\tgive one entity two contradictory fact sets and is not permitted.\n   251\t\n   252\tAn occurrence is the pair `[source-eid lexical-context]`, where\n   253\t`lexical-context` is the complete innermost-first stack of enclosing\n   254\tparameter vectors, exactly the memo key the dormant projection documents.\n   255\t`resolve` mints one resolved record per distinct occurrence, with a fresh\n   256\tnegative id assigned deterministically in first-visit order of the walk,\n   257\tand reuses that record wherever the same occurrence recurs. Two references\n   258\tto one entity under equal contexts share one record, so graph sharing is\n   259\tpreserved; two references under unequal contexts get two records. A shared\n   260\tlambda under two contexts yields two resolved lambdas, each with its own\n   261\tresolved body. Resolved ids never equal source ids; provenance is a side\n   262\ttable, below. A cyclic input is refused, as the projection refuses it.\n   263\t\n   264\tEach resolved record is the source node's datoms with exactly two changes,\n   265\tthe same two changes the B1 opcode table makes to the named instruction\n   266\ttable. Every other datom is carried unchanged: `:yin/type`, exact scalar\n   267\tvalues, exact free names, `:yin/tail?`, and the node's operand references,\n   268\twhich now point at resolved record ids.\n   269\t\n   270\t1. A `:variable` record has no `:yin/name` datom and has either\n   271\t   `:yin.resolved/depth` and `:yin.resolved/position` (a bound reference,\n   272\t   from `resolve-name`'s `{:bound [depth position]}`) or `:yin.resolved/free`\n   273\t   carrying the exact symbol (from `{:free name}`).\n   274\t2. A `:lambda` record has no `:yin/params` datom and has\n   275\t   `:yin.resolved/arity`, the parameter count.\n   276\t\n   277\tThe attribute namespace is `:yin.resolved/*`, not `:yin.debruijn/*`, which\n   278\tthe dormant projection owns. The side table has two maps: `:source`,\n   279\t`{resolved-id source-eid}` for every record, and `:params`,\n   280\t`{resolved-lambda-id [param-symbol ...]}`, the exact parameter vector of\n   281\tevery resolved lambda. No name is recorded per variable occurrence; a bound\n   282\toccurrence's original spelling is the owning resolved lambda's parameter at\n   283\tthe resolved position, which `resolve-name`'s contract guarantees is exact.\n   284\tBinder names appear nowhere in the resolved tuples, including in ids.\n   285\t\n   286\tThe walk is in the named linearizer's order: operator then operands left to\n   287\tright, `if` test then arms, lambda bodies with the lambda's parameter vector\n   288\tpushed innermost-first on the resolution stack for the body's extent. Depth\n   289\t0 is the innermost enclosing lambda. The resolution stack and the occurrence\n   290\tmemo are the resolver's only state; the runtime frame vector's outermost-\n   291\tfirst order is B3's, and the conversion between them is explicit there.\n   292\t\n   293\t`resolve` refuses with a qualified diagnostic, before any lowerer runs, a\n   294\tprogram whose bound reference falls outside its enclosing arity chain, an\n   295\tunsupported node type, a host value in a data operand, or a cycle. The\n   296\tresolver also exports one pure `validate-resolved` over an already resolved\n   297\ttuple set and side table: shape, one resolution fact set per record, every\n   298\tbound reference inside its enclosing arity chain, every operand reference\n   299\ta record, and `:source` total over the records. Every lowerer calls it\n   300\tunconditionally on its input, so a hand-built resolved set with\n   301\t`:yin.resolved/depth 5` and no enclosing lambda is refused identically by\n   302\t`lower-stack` and by the register lowerer, before either emits anything.\n   303\t\n   304\tThe inverse `unresolve` maps each record back through `:source`, restores\n   305\t`:yin/name` from the owning lambda's parameters and `:yin/params` from\n   306\t`:params`, and merges the records of one source entity, which carry equal\n   307\tfacts by construction. `unresolve(resolve x, side-table)` equals `x` as a\n   308\tdatom set, including the shared-entity fixtures.\n   309\t\n   310\tThe resolved tuples are a derived stage value. They have this shape\n   311\tcontract and namespace so that two lowerers and their tests agree on what\n   312\tthey consume, but no hash, no identity, and no sharing role: H and R are\n   313\tthe only executable identities (D9, D12), the tuples are not executable,\n   314\tand \"derive, do not persist\" forbids a persisted copy of facts the named\n   315\tdatoms already hold. They are not the second lossless view D2 rejects:\n   316\tnever stored as a source view, never served, never a fetch key. With the\n   317\tbinder side table they invert to the named datoms; that inverse is the\n   318\tresolver's test oracle only.\n   319\t\n   320\t### 3.2 Stack lowering\n   321\t\n   322\t`lower-stack` consumes resolved tuples and their side table, calls\n   323\t`validate-resolved` first and refuses on its diagnostic, then emits the\n   324\tcanonical positional `:yin.debruijn.code/*` image (section 2) plus the\n   325\tpc-keyed diagnostic side table the lift needs. It cannot call\n   326\t`yin.vm.linearize/lower`, which reads `:yin/name` and `:yin/params`. It\n   327\ttherefore reproduces the named linearizer's flattening itself: the same\n   328\trecursion in evaluation order, the same `:push` after operator and after\n   329\teach operand, `:call` with argc and the copied `:yin/tail?`, `if` as\n   330\t`branch-false`, `jump`, and two labels, `:halt` after the root, lambda\n   331\tbodies out of line in discovery order each ending in `:return`, and labels\n   332\tresolved to pcs in a second pass. Like `lower`, it expands occurrences\n   333\tpositionally: a resolved record referenced twice is emitted twice, at two\n   334\tpcs; the resolver's occurrence memo never changes emitted bytes. A\n   335\t`:variable` emits `[:load-bound depth position]` or `[:load-free name]`\n   336\tstraight from its resolved attributes; a `:lambda` emits `[:closure arity\n   337\tbody-pc]`. Every other node emits the carried mnemonic unchanged.\n   338\t\n   339\tLayout equality with the named linearizer no longer holds by construction.\n   340\tIt is a required structural-comparison test: for every corpus program the\n   341\tcanonical vector of `(lower x)` and `(lower-stack (resolve x))` have the\n   342\tsame length and the same mnemonic at every pc, and differ only at `:var`\n   343\tversus `:load-bound`/`:load-free` operands and at `:closure`'s parameter\n   344\tversus arity operand. A layout change in `yin.vm.linearize` must fail this\n   345\ttest rather than silently fork H.\n   346\t\n   347\t`lower-stack` copies `:yin/tail?` exactly as it was resolved. It does not\n   348\tinfer tail position from syntax. Only `:application` has a tail operand in\n   349\tthe existing instruction table; `:dao.stream.apply/call` lowers to\n   350\t`:ffi-call` and has no tail operand.\n   351\t\n   352\tThe pc side table is `{pc {:kind :closure, :params [sym ...], :source e}}`\n   353\tfor every `:closure` pc, with `:params` read from the resolver side table's\n   354\t`:params` by resolved lambda id, and `{pc {:kind :var, :source e}}` for\n   355\tevery load pc. `:source` is the source eid, read through the resolver side\n   500\tapplication, `if`, stream, store, gensym, continuation, park, resume, and FFI\n   501\toperations. Macro expansion remains upstream; unexpanded macro diagnostics\n   502\tare not runtime execution.\n   503\t\n   504\tEquivalence excludes the named VM's stale-register leak. A named VM instance\n   505\tused for B5 must begin with a fixed initial environment, or the test records a\n   506\tnamed-path defect and does not claim parity. It also excludes representation\n   507\tdetails that the normalizer intentionally erases. It does not exclude exact\n   508\tliteral values, spelling-sensitive store keys, front-end tail choices, stream\n   509\toutcomes, or error classifications.\n   510\t\n   511\t## 6. Implementation phases\n   512\t\n   513\tEach phase B0 through B7 has a file box, a must-not-change list, completion\n   514\tcriteria, and JVM, Node/CLJS, ClojureDart, kondo, and cljstyle verification.\n   515\tThe shortest sharing path is B0, B1, B2, B6, then B3 through B5, then B7.\n   516\tB6 depends on B1, B2, and the lift, not on B3 or B4; it may execute a fetched\n   517\timage through the existing semantic VM after lifting with synthesized names.\n   518\t\n   519\t### B0: contract and normalizer\n   520\t\n   521\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc\n   522\t    Existing edits: none\n   523\t    Must not change: AST, emitter, merged projection namespace, named VM,\n   524\t    code dimension\n   525\t\n   526\tFreeze the result/error normalizer and actual parity corpus:\n   527\t`parity-test` plus the every-tag corpora in `content_test` and\n   528\t`completion_test`. Completion requires named-VM self-parity and idempotence of\n   529\tthe normalizer, plus normalized closure, continuation, error, stream, cursor,\n   530\tand store comparisons, duplicate-parameter, and all-node fixtures.\n   531\t\n   532\t### B1: executable dimension and validator\n   533\t\n   534\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc\n   535\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc\n   536\t    Existing edits: none\n   537\t    Must not change: yin.vm.code, :yin.code/*, merged projection namespace\n   538\t\n   539\tDefine and export the descriptor, derived opcode table, exact executable\n   540\tscalar encoder, code-image hash, and validator. Completion includes malformed\n   541\trows,\n   542\tout-of-range bound operands, exact spelling preservation, distinct hashes\n   543\tfor composed and decomposed e-acute, a ratio fixture that is either\n   544\tencoded or refused with `:unsupported-value` (JVM encodes, CLJS and\n   545\tClojureDart both refuse), distinct hashes for `1`/`1.0` on JVM and Dart,\n   546\tdistinct hashes for ratios and chars on the JVM, and identical bytes\n   547\tacross hosts for the common scalar domain. Golden image bytes and H\n   548\tvalues for a frozen corpus are checked on all three host lanes. A\n   549\tlowering layout change must fail those fixtures rather than silently\n   550\tforking identity.\n    55\tregister path adds no projection fingerprint and no global registry. It\n    56\tproposes one change to the stack design's B2 file box, recorded in section\n    57\t2.2 and deferred to the owner; everything else in `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`\n    58\tis unchanged.\n    59\t\n    60\t### 1.1 Identity and sharing\n    61\t\n    62\tThe register descriptor is `:yin.debruijn.register/*`. Its `register-hash`\n    63\tfunction, R, hashes the register descriptor hash and the canonical\n    64\tpositional register vector, exactly as B1's `image-hash` hashes the stack\n    65\tdescriptor hash and the canonical stack vector. R is the only identity used\n    66\tto fetch, verify, cache, or execute register bytes. A linker request must\n    67\tname both the format and R, for example\n    68\t`{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash R}`.\n    69\t\n    70\tR is alpha-invariant for binder renames for the same reason H is: the\n    71\tregister vector carries `:load-bound` depth and position operands and\n    72\t`:closure` arities, and binder names live in a diagnostic side table outside\n    73\tthe hash. Equal R therefore implies agreement on exact scalar spelling, free\n    74\tname operands, and front-end tail flags, as equal H does. Each format is\n    75\tdeterministically derived from the resolved tuples, so equal resolved\n    76\ttuples give equal H and equal R, and a binder rename changes neither. No\n    77\tbiconditional between H and R is claimed: two deterministic projections may\n    78\tpreserve different distinctions, and the corpus tests are evidence for the\n    79\tone-directional law only.\n    80\t\n    81\tThis is not a second semantic answer to alpha-equivalence. A register\n    82\tlowerer cannot repair differences in exact scalar spelling, free names, or\n    83\tfront-end tail flags; those differences already make the resolved tuples,\n    84\tand so both H and R, differ.\n    85\t\n    86\tThe pairing of an R with the H of the same named root is a composition\n    87\tfact, carried beside a request or recorded as a datom by whoever publishes\n    88\tboth, never inside either preimage. A receiver verifies a fetched register\n    89\timage with R alone and never needs to have seen the stack image.\n    90\t\n    91\t## 2. Compilation topology and stream contract\n    92\t\n    93\tThe register projection is a peer of the stack projection. Both branch from\n    94\tthe resolved tuples, the de Bruijn encoding of the named AST datoms:\n    95\t\n    96\t    Source -> Yang -> named AST datoms (:yin/*)\n    97\t                         |\\\n    98\t                         | \\-> named semantic image (:yin.code/*)\n    99\t                         |\n   100\t                         +----> resolved tuples (de Bruijn AST, no H)\n   101\t                                   |\\\n   102\t                                   | \\-> stack image (H)      B1 shape, B2\n   103\t                                   |\n   104\t                                   +---> register image (R)   R1\n   105\t\n   106\tThe register edge is not chained after the stack image. The named stream,\n   107\tthe resolved tuples, the stack image, and the register image coexist and\n   108\tremain independently queryable or persistable, per the pipeline invariant\n   109\tthat no stage destroys its input.\n   110\t\n   111\tThe stages have the pure shapes:\n   112\t\n   113\t    (resolve named-datom-stream)      -> resolved-tuple-stream\n   114\t    (lower-stack resolved-stream)     -> stack-image-stream     (H)\n   115\t    (lower-register resolved-stream)  -> register-image-stream  (R)\n   116\t\n   117\tEach register output item carries a register descriptor reference, the\n   118\tcanonical register vector, R, and a diagnostic side table. The implementation\n   119\tmay build indexes while evaluating one bounded item, but those indexes are\n   120\tlocal function data and are not written back into any input.\n   121\t\n   122\tThe register stage refuses malformed resolved tuples, unsupported node\n   123\ttypes, or out-of-range bound operands with qualified data diagnostics. It\n   124\tdoes not silently fall back inside the lowerer. A composition may route the\n   125\tsame named root to the stack path after receiving that outcome.\n   126\t\n   127\t### 2.1 The shared artifact: resolved tuples\n   128\t\n   129\tThe resolved tuples are defined by the stack design, section 3.1, and that\n   130\tdefinition is normative here. In summary: the named `:yin/*` datoms form a\n   131\tfinite graph in which one source entity may be referenced under several\n   132\tlexical contexts, and resolution is a fact about an occurrence, the pair\n   133\t`[source-eid lexical-context]`, not about the entity. The resolver mints one\n   134\tresolved record per distinct occurrence, with a fresh deterministic id, and\n   135\tshares it across references under equal contexts. Each record is the source\n   136\tnode's datoms with exactly two changes, the same two changes B1's opcode\n   137\ttable makes to the named instruction table:\n   138\t\n   139\t1. A `:variable` record is resolved by the public\n   140\t   `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` against the innermost-first stack of\n   141\t   enclosing parameter vectors, to either a bound reference `[depth\n   142\t   position]` or a free reference carrying the exact symbol.\n   143\t2. A `:lambda` record carries its arity instead of its parameter vector.\n   144\t\n   145\tEverything else is carried exactly: scalar values with their exact spelling,\n   146\tfree names as supplied, `:yin/tail?` as the front end wrote it, node\n   147\tstructure, and evaluation order. Binder names and provenance go to the\n   148\tresolver's side table, keyed by resolved record: `:source` maps every\n   149\trecord to its source eid and `:params` maps every resolved lambda to its\n   150\texact parameter vector. Nothing is canonicalized: this is not the dormant\n   151\tmerged projection, which canonicalizes scalars, NFC-folds names, and drops\n   152\ttail flags, and which the stack design's architecture B rejected as a\n   153\tlowering input for those reasons. It does share that projection's\n   154\toccurrence key, because the two face the same graph.\n   155\t\n   156\tThe resolver is the graph-level home of name resolution. It validates each\n   157\tbound reference against the enclosing arity chain, the same nonnegative-\n   158\tdepth-and-position rule B2 checks on the vector, refuses a program whose\n   159\tresolution fails before either lowerer runs, and exports the one\n   160\t`validate-resolved` both lowerers call unconditionally on their input. It\n   161\treuses only `resolve-name`; projection-only helpers are not reused, and the\n   162\tresolver inherits the stack design's retirement condition for that helper.\n   163\t\n   164\tThe resolved tuples have a shape contract and a namespace so that two\n   165\tlowerers and their tests can agree on what they consume. They have no hash,\n   166\tno identity, and no sharing role:\n   167\t\n   168\t- Invariant I shares executable code. The resolved tuples are not executable\n   169\t  by any VM in this project; the executable artifacts are the stack image\n   170\t  and the register image, identified by H and R.\n   171\t- D9 and D12 make H the only identity on the stack sharing path, computed by\n   172\t  one function over the descriptor hash and the canonical vector. R mirrors\n   173\t  that rule for the register path. A hash of the resolved tuples would be a\n   174\t  third identity that no request, response, verification, or cache needs.\n   175\t- \"Derive, do not persist\" (`datom.world.md`): the resolved tuples are a\n   176\t  pure function of the named datoms and `resolve-name`. Persisting them or\n   177\t  giving them an identity would duplicate authority over facts the named\n   178\t  datoms already hold. They are a stage value: computed, consumed, and\n   179\t  discardable. A composition that chooses to retain the stream may do so,\n   180\t  as it may retain any intermediate, but nothing downstream keys on it.\n   181\t- D2 and section 7.3 of the stack design forbid a second lossless record\n   182\t  DAG. The resolved tuples are not one: they are never stored as a source\n   183\t  view, never served, and never a fetch key. With the side table they are\n   184\t  invertible to the named datoms, but that property is used only by tests.\n   185\t\n   186\t### 2.2 Relation to B2 as implemented\n   187\t\n   188\tB2 today produces the stack image in one fused pass: it runs the named\n   189\tlinearizer `yin.vm.linearize/lower`, converts the result to the canonical\n   190\tnamed vector, reconstructs body scopes from that vector, and rewrites `:var`\n   191\tto `:load-bound` or `:load-free` and `:closure` parameters to arity. Its de\n   192\tBruijn addressing therefore happens after stack-shape assembly, over an\n   193\talready push-and-argc shaped vector. There is no point inside B2 at which an\n   194\texecution-model-agnostic de Bruijn form exists.\n   195\t\n   196\tThe peer topology above does not require that file box to change in order\n   197\tto ship R0-R2. B2's fused pass and the two-stage path `lower-stack` after\n   198\t`resolve` are extensionally equal: the named linearizer's walk order,\n   199\toccurrence expansion, and body layout are what `lower-stack` reproduces,\n   200\tand resolution commutes with that walk because it reads only the enclosing\n   201\tparameter stack. The register design therefore binds the two by a tested\n   202\tlaw rather than by construction:\n   203\t\n   204\t    addresses(B2 stack image) = addresses(register image)\n   205\t                              = addresses(resolved tuples)\n   206\t\n   207\twhere `addresses` is the sequence of resolved variable references, bound\n   208\t`[depth position]` or free name, in evaluation order with occurrences\n   209\texpanded positionally as both lowerers expand them: the main sequence\n   210\tfirst, then each out-of-line body in discovery order, a record referenced\n   211\ttwice contributing twice. The register lowerer pins that same body layout\n   212\torder so the three sequences align position by position. This law is an R0\n   213\tfixture over the whole B0 parity corpus plus the shared-occurrence fixtures\n   214\tof the stack design's B2 box.\n   215\t\n   216\tRecommended, and deferred to the owner because it edits the stack design's\n   217\tsection 3 and B2 box: refactor B2, while it is still uncommitted, into\n   218\t`resolve` followed by a stack linearization of the resolved tuples. That\n   219\tremoves B2's vector-level scope reconstruction (`closure-body-ranges`,\n   220\t`layout-conforms?`, `body-owner`, `chain-of`), which exists only because\n   221\tresolution was placed after linearization, and makes the stack image\n   222\tliterally a peer projection rather than an equivalent one. The cost is a\n   223\tcopy of the named linearizer's flattening walk in the stack lowerer, since\n   224\t`lower` cannot consume resolved tuples, and the loss of by-construction\n   225\tlayout equality with `lower`, which becomes the existing structural-\n   245\t    | Validation           | Shape, targets, body scope, register bounds   |\n   246\t    | Lift                 | Register image to named semantics             |\n   247\t    +----------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n   248\t\n   249\tThe register descriptor has its own validator because register operands and\n   250\tbody register counts are new facts. It reuses B1's public data rules and\n   251\tscalar encoder only through an explicit dependency; it does not call private\n   252\tprojection helpers. The descriptor version is incremented whenever opcode\n   253\tshape, allocation, scalar framing, or control-flow rules change.\n   254\t\n   255\tThe lowerer's R formula is:\n   256\t\n   257\t    R = sha256(register-descriptor-hash || canonical-register-vector)\n   258\t\n   259\tBoth components are canonical bytes. Received register bytes are hashed\n   260\tbefore decoding, as in B1's wire rule. A receiver verifies R, checks the\n   261\tdescriptor, runs the receiver closure check over `:load-free` operands\n   262\texactly as D11 and D15 define it for stack images, validates, and only then\n   263\texecutes. No relation to a stack H is embedded or checked.\n   264\t\n   265\tThe lift goes from the register image to the named `:yin.code/*` image, so\n   266\tthat B6's rule \"a fetched image may be executed by the semantic VM after\n   267\tlifting\" holds for register images too. It is a function of the register\n   268\timage plus its side table, under the stack design's trust rule (section\n   269\t3.2 there): execution lifts use synthesized names, fresh against the\n   270\timage's free-name set and each other, by default. A supplied side table is\n   271\tused only when every closure entry's parameter count equals the\n   272\tinstruction's arity, its names are capture-free by the same rule, and\n   273\tlowering the lifted result reproduces the original register image byte for\n   274\tbyte; otherwise it is discarded. R1 tests original-name equality and\n   275\tsynthesized-name alpha-equivalence, including duplicate parameters.\n   276\t\n   277\t## 4. Resolved-tuples-to-register lowering\n   278\t\n   279\t### 4.1 Input and validation\n   280\t\n   281\tThe lowerer accepts only resolved tuples from the section 2.1 resolver and\n   282\ttheir side table. It calls the resolver's `validate-resolved` first and\n   283\trefuses on its diagnostic, identically to `lower-stack`, before emitting\n   284\tanything. It does not accept named datoms directly, does not rerun name\n   285\tresolution, and does not accept a stack image. B2 remains the only\n   380\t(`yin.vm.engine.md`: one restore function, per-site park-entry builders, the\n   381\tengine-owned key sets). It adds no `IVM` or `IVMState` methods.\n   382\t\n   383\tThe register kernel must execute only validator-approved images. It must\n   384\tpreserve B3's frame direction, closure capture, nil-fill and extra-argument\n   385\trules, and B0 normalization. Register continuations are not interchangeable\n   386\twith stack continuations unless an explicit lift is provided.\n   387\t\n   388\t## 6. Implementation phases\n   389\t\n   390\tEach phase has a bounded file box, must-not-change list, completion criteria,\n   391\tand JVM, CLJS, CLJD, kondo, and cljstyle verification.\n   392\t\n   393\t### R0: contract and corpus\n   394\t\n   395\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_contract_test.cljc\n   396\t    Existing edits: none\n   397\t    Must not change: B0-B3, named datoms, stack image, resolver contract,\n   398\t    projection namespace\n   399\t\n   400\tThe resolver (`yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`: resolved tuples, side table,\n   401\tscope validation, the inverse used by tests) is now B2's own file box, per\n   402\tthe stack design's B2 phase box (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 3.1) --\n   403\tnot built here. R0 depends on it and freezes the register descriptor\n   404\tversion, operand mapping, normalizer fixtures, and the named-root corpus\n   405\tagainst it. Completion requires every node type from the resolver either\n   406\tmapped or refused with a named diagnostic, the duplicate-parameter and\n   407\tfree-name fixtures from B2, the section 2.2 address law between B2's stack\n   408\timages and the resolved tuples over the B0 parity corpus, pure data\n   409\tchecks, and a decision that no register kernel is assumed yet.\n   410\t\n   411\t### R1: register dimension and lowerer\n   412\t\n   413\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc\n   414\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc\n   415\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:72:`:closure` arities, and binder names live in a diagnostic side table outside\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:77:biconditional between H and R is claimed: two deterministic projections may\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:114:    (lower-stack resolved-stream)     -> stack-image-stream     (H)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:115:    (lower-register resolved-stream)  -> register-image-stream  (R)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:118:canonical register vector, R, and a diagnostic side table. The implementation\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:132:lexical contexts, and resolution is a fact about an occurrence, the pair\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:133:`[source-eid lexical-context]`, not about the entity. The resolver mints one\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:134:resolved record per distinct occurrence, with a fresh deterministic id, and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:146:free names as supplied, `:yin/tail